README-NETPBM -- the image converters, and how to see them workCMDS/NETPBM holds netpbm: programs that convert and transform images.SYS/login puts the directory on the PATH.  Every program's original manualpage is in DOC/netpbm, and is the reference for its options:    nroff -man /dd/DOC/netpbm/pnmscale.1This file says what they are for, and what trips people up.  None of themneeds a graphics screen; pbmtoascii shows any image on a terminal.TRY IT  pbmtext os9 | pbmtoasciineeds no file: pbmtext draws the word as an image, pbmtoascii prints itas characters.  Two images ship in DEMO:  pnmfile /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm  pnminvert /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pgmtopbm -threshold -value 0.5 | pbmtoascii  giftopnm /dd/DEMO/gulls.gif | pnmfile  giftopnm /dd/DEMO/gulls.gif | ppmtopgm | pnmscale -width 78 | pgmtopbm | pbmtoasciiThe sphere prints as a filled circle.  It is plain (ASCII) PGM, so you canread it too: the magic number P2, width and height, the maximum greyvalue, then one number per pixel.  gulls.gif is a photograph, so itsdithering is busy.  pnmscale 0.5 /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pnmfile        halve it  pnmflip -cw /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pnmfile         turn it  pnmsmooth /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pnmfile           blur itA MISSING ARGUMENT READS AS A BAD FILEGiven only a filename, pnmscale takes the name as its scale factor andreads an empty standard input: "bad magic number - not a ppm, pgm, or pbmfile".  Many of these do the same.  That message means an argument ismissing, not that the file or the program is bad.CONVERTING BETWEEN FORMATSEach converter is named XtoY: giftopnm reads GIF and writes PNM, ppmtogifgoes back.  These formats have been round-tripped, out and back, with theimage coming back the same size:  atk cmuwm gem mgr xbm ybm g3 bmp gif ilbm pict pj tga xpm  fits rast sir xwd fs icon macp pi3 pi1 pcx sgi yuv lispmFour have a fixed canvas and change the size by design: MacPaint isalways 576 by 720, Atari ST PI3 640 by 400, PI1 320 by 200, and an X11icon pads its width to a whole number of bytes.  * pcxtoppm and sgitopnm seek backwards in their input, so they cannot    read a pipe ("error seeking past header").  Give them a file:        ppmtopcx < in.ppm > /dd/tmp/x.pcx ; pcxtoppm /dd/tmp/x.pcx  * yuvtoppm needs the width and height; the file does not carry them:        ppmtoyuv < in.ppm | yuvtoppm 32 16  * lispmtopgm in this build takes at most 16 grey levels ("depth is too    large - try reconfiguring with PGM_BIGGRAYS").  Reduce first:        pnmdepth 15 < in.pgm | pgmtolispm | lispmtopgm  * pbmtopk and pktopbm are for TeX fonts.  They take filenames and a    resolution, not a pipe, and pbmtopk also wants a .tfm metric file,    which this disk does not have.These read formats nothing on this disk writes, and no sample file ships,so none has been run on a file of its own format:  bioradtopgm brushtopbm gouldtoppm hipstopgm hpcdtoppm imgtoppm  mtvtoppm psidtopgm qrttoppm sldtoppm spctoppm spottopgm sputoppm  ximtoppm xvminitoppm zeisstopnmJPEG: CR AND LF IN THE HEADERcjpeg and djpeg work, but not straight through a pipe with netpbm: theyend the three lines of a PNM header with LF, and netpbm here uses CR.Each side blames the data:    cjpeg:     Bogus data in PPM file    ppmtopgm:  junk in file where an integer should bepbyte fixes the three separators in place.  Out to JPEG, CR becomes LF:    ppmmake red 8 8 > red.ppm    pbyte red.ppm 2 0a    pbyte red.ppm 6 0a          `8 8' is three characters    pbyte red.ppm a 0a    cjpeg red.ppm > red.jpgand back, LF becomes CR:    djpeg -pnm red.jpg > back.ppm    pbyte back.ppm 2 0d    pbyte back.ppm 6 0d    pbyte back.ppm a 0d    pnmfile back.ppm            PPM raw, 8 by 8The offsets move with the digits in the dimensions -- count them.  Forthe JPEG library's 227x149 test image they are 2, A and E.  DOC/STATUShas more.TAKING AN IMAGE OFF THE EMULATORos9exec turns CR into CRLF on output to a host file, so a raw imageredirected out gains a byte at every pixel whose value is 13.  Pipesinside OS-9 are clean.  Convert to plain (ASCII) format with pnmnorawbefore taking an image elsewhere.COLOUR NAMES: TWO COPIES OF rgb.txtPrograms that take a colour by name -- `ppmmake blue', `pgmtoppmred-blue', ppmchange -- read the X11 colour database.  It ships twice:    /dd/SYS/rgb.txt         the usual name    /dd/SYS/rgb.txt.txt     the name the binaries openPPM/libppm4.c appends ".txt" to RGB_DB, and this port defined RGB_DB as`/dd/sys/rgb.txt', extension included.  Without the second copy they stopwith "can't open color names database - reconfigure with correct RGBDEF".`ppmmake blue 3 2' gives 0 0 255 and `ppmmake "light steel blue" 2 1'gives 176 196 222, the X11 values.  If you rebuild from SRC/netpbm,define RGB_DB as `/dd/SYS/rgb' and one copy is enough.The database is SRC/netpbm/VMS/RGB.txt, from the same archive.